---
/**
 * Who writes this.
 *
 * The audit called this the single biggest humanising change available, and
 * the reasoning holds: a site whose whole argument is "a person went and
 * checked" needs the person to be findable. No stock photo, no invented
 * credentials, no claim to be a local. What's here is checkable, which is the
 * same standard the rest of the site is held to.
 */
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";

const schema = [
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "AboutPage",
    mainEntity: {
      "@type": "Person",
      name: "Matt Werth",
      jobTitle: "Software engineer",
      worksFor: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Werth Design", url: "https://werth.design" },
      description:
        "Software engineer and photographer. Travels the US to see parks, and writes and checks the Deep Creek Field Guide.",
    },
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="Who writes this — Deep Creek Field Guide"
  description="Matt Werth writes and checks this site. Software engineer and photographer, travels the US to see parks, publishes the mistakes."
  schema={schema}
>
  <div class="wrap pt-11 pb-2">
    <p class="label m-0">About</p>
    <h1 class="text-[2.2rem] sm:text-[2.9rem] mt-3.5 mb-0 max-w-[14ch] text-slate">Who writes this</h1>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap prose-field pb-4">
    <p class="text-[1.2rem] leading-[1.5] font-medium max-w-[56ch]">
      Matt Werth. Just me, not a team.
    </p>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      I'm a software engineer and a photographer, and I travel all over the US
      to look at parks. Garrett County isn't home. I drive out from Delaware,
      usually with two kids in the back and most of their belongings in the
      trunk.
    </p>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      So I'm not local. That's partly why the site runs on phone calls and
      source documents instead of on what I remember. The photographs are mine
      though, which is the one part I can vouch for personally.
    </p>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      I run <a href="https://werth.design" rel="noopener">Werth Design</a>, a
      studio in Wilmington, and it pays for all this. Nothing here is sponsored
      today. If that changes, the page it changes on will say so.
    </p>

    <h2>Why I built it</h2>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      I drove to a park with a car full of kids and the thing I'd read about
      turned out to be wrong. That's the whole origin story. Not dramatic. It
      happens to everybody.
    </p>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      And the wrong fact wasn't really the annoying part. It was that I couldn't
      tell how old the answer was, or who'd said it in the first place. A page
      written in 2019 looks exactly like a page written last week.
    </p>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      So every fact here carries a date and a source. Where I haven't checked
      something, it says so.
    </p>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      That makes the site look thinner than the ones it's up against. Fine by
      me.
    </p>

    <h2>How I work</h2>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      I ring whoever sets the rule. Maryland DNR for a state park, the town for
      a town park, the organizer for an event. Then I write down the date and
      who I spoke to.
    </p>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      If I only read something off an official page and didn't actually speak to
      anyone, the page says that instead. They're different claims and you
      should get to see which one you've got.
    </p>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      Being an engineer, this ends up mostly tables. I don't trust my own memory
      of a park I saw two summers ago. (You shouldn't either.)
    </p>

    <h2>I get things wrong in public</h2>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      Three so far, all still up with the date and what I used to say. The
      Swallow Falls reservation rule. Swimming at the 4-H center, which I had as
      open to anybody when it's actually for camp programs. And three parks I'd
      marked "believed free", which is just a guess in a nicer sweater.
    </p>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      <a href="/how-we-verify/">All three are on the method page</a>. They stay
      up. A guide that promises checking and then quietly edits its mistakes
      away is worse than one that never promised anything.
    </p>

    <h2>Tell me when I'm wrong</h2>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      There's a report box at the bottom of every park page. Takes about ten
      seconds. People who live there know these places far better than I do, and
      one note from someone who was standing in the parking lot yesterday is worth
      more than another phone call from me.
    </p>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      Most of what's been fixed lately came in that way.
    </p>

    <h2>Related</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/how-we-verify/">How I check things, and what I've got wrong</a></li>
      <li><a href="/verified/">Every fact on the site, with its source and age</a></li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</Layout>
